IMPORT prelude

-- Singapore Armed Forces Act 1972 (Singapore) -- who is subject to military law (s 3); which offences a disciplinary officer may
-- try summarily (ss 4, 61, First Schedule); absence without leave, desertion and failing to report them (ss 22, 23, 24) and the
-- maximum terms for a handful of common offences (ss 11, 15, 21, 25, 32, 33, 34); the right to elect a court martial (s 77);
-- time limits for summary trial (s 78) and for any trial (s 111); composition (s 79A); trial twice (s 108); murder and culpable
-- homicide (s 112(2)); and assignment of military pay and pensions (s 196).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/SAFA1972.txt, with
-- amendments to Act 9 of 2026 (in force 1 May 2026) shown. THE ARRANGEMENT OF SECTIONS IN THE .txt DEPOSIT IS ONE NUMBER OUT OF
-- STEP WITH THE BODY (it lists "21. Absence without leave"; the body has it at s 22). The body's numbers are used throughout. THE
-- FIRST SCHEDULE TABLE IS COLUMN-SHIFTED: section numbers were matched to items by order of appearance (items 1-15 to ss 16, 17,
-- 19, 20, 21, 22, 24, 25, 26, 27, 28, 29, 30, 32, 33; items 16-28 to ss 42-48, 50(a) and (b), 51, 52, 53, 57, 58).
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Cited by 18 of the 527 Singapore Acts deposited in this repository. SCOPED to the rules a serviceman, a national
-- serviceman or a defence lawyer meets first: who is subject, how an absence is charged, who may try it and for how long the
-- charge stays alive. Not encoded: organisation and appointments (Part 2), most of the 50-odd offences in Part 3, the
-- disciplinary officers' powers of punishment (ss 63-75), court-martial constitution and procedure (Part 5), the scale of
-- punishments (s 118), the Military Court of Appeal (Part 7), arrest, search, bail, boards of inquiry, pay deductions, emergency
-- powers and aid to civilian authorities (Parts 8-12A), and every regulation made under s 205 (none was read).
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Singapore Armed Forces Act 1972 -- discipline`

§§ `3 -- who is subject to military law`

-- s 3: subject to military law are (a) "regular servicemen and full-time national servicemen ... from the time their liability to
-- report for enlistment or service arises until such time as they are lawfully discharged or released"; (b) operationally ready
-- national servicemen "from the time that they are ordered to report for enlistment or service, whether they have complied with
-- such order or not", and while in uniform or performing service, mobilised, or called out in aid of the civil power; (c)
-- civilians in the service of the SAF "when engaged on active service"; (d) civilians who are followers of, or accompany, the SAF
-- when engaged on active service; (e) Commonwealth or foreign forces when attached to or acting with the SAF; (f) volunteers
-- "during the period that they are ordered to report for service, whether they have complied with such order or not".
-- The several triggers in (b) are collapsed into one flag.
DECLARE Category IS ONE OF
    `a regular serviceman`
    `a full-time national serviceman`
    `an operationally ready national serviceman`
    `a civilian in the service of the SAF`
    `a civilian accompanying the SAF`
    `a member of a foreign force`
    `a volunteer`
    `a member of the public`

DECLARE Person
    HAS `category` IS A Category
        `liability to report has arisen` IS A BOOLEAN
        `lawfully discharged or released` IS A BOOLEAN
        `ordered to report, in uniform or performing service` IS A BOOLEAN
        `the SAF is engaged on active service` IS A BOOLEAN
        `attached to or acting with the SAF` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `p` IS A Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is subject to military law` IF
    CONSIDER `p`'s `category`
    WHEN `a regular serviceman` THEN `p`'s `liability to report has arisen` AND (NOT `p`'s `lawfully discharged or released`)
    WHEN `a full-time national serviceman` THEN `p`'s `liability to report has arisen` AND (NOT `p`'s `lawfully discharged or released`)
    WHEN `an operationally ready national serviceman` THEN `p`'s `ordered to report, in uniform or performing service`
    WHEN `a volunteer` THEN `p`'s `ordered to report, in uniform or performing service`
    WHEN `a civilian in the service of the SAF` THEN `p`'s `the SAF is engaged on active service`
    WHEN `a civilian accompanying the SAF` THEN `p`'s `the SAF is engaged on active service`
    WHEN `a member of a foreign force` THEN `p`'s `attached to or acting with the SAF`
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `Part 3 -- the offences encoded`

-- Maximum terms on conviction by a subordinate military court, each "or any less punishment authorised by this Act":
--   s 11 misconduct in action -- "death or any less punishment" (no term of years stated);
--   s 15 mutiny -- 10 years, but death where committed in the face of the enemy or involving violence (see below);
--   s 21 disobedience of general orders -- 2 years;   s 22 absence without leave -- 2 years;   s 23 desertion -- 10 years;
--   s 24 failure to report deserters and absentees -- 2 years;   s 25 conduct to the prejudice of good order -- 12 months;
--   s 32 malingering -- 12 months;   s 33 intoxication -- 6 months;   s 34 drugs or psychoactive substances -- 5 years.
DECLARE Offence IS ONE OF
    `misconduct in action`
    `mutiny`
    `disobedience of general orders`
    `absence without leave`
    `desertion`
    `failure to report deserters and absentees`
    `conduct to the prejudice of good order`
    `malingering`
    `intoxication`
    `drug possession or consumption`

-- Misconduct in action carries death; no term of years is stated, so 0 is returned and should not be read as a term.
GIVEN `o` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum term in months` MEANS
    CONSIDER `o`
    WHEN `misconduct in action` THEN 0
    WHEN `mutiny` THEN 120
    WHEN `disobedience of general orders` THEN 24
    WHEN `absence without leave` THEN 24
    WHEN `desertion` THEN 120
    WHEN `failure to report deserters and absentees` THEN 24
    WHEN `conduct to the prejudice of good order` THEN 12
    WHEN `malingering` THEN 12
    WHEN `intoxication` THEN 6
    WHEN `drug possession or consumption` THEN 60

-- s 15(1): for a mutiny involving violence or aimed at avoiding service against the enemy, "if the offence is committed in the
-- face of the enemy or involves the use of violence he or she SHALL be punished with death". Otherwise 10 years (s 15(1), (2)).
GIVEN `in the face of the enemy` IS A BOOLEAN, `involved violence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `death is mandatory for the mutiny` IF
    `in the face of the enemy` OR `involved violence`

-- s 22(1): absent without leave "from service ... or from the place where he or she is lawfully required for the time being to
-- be". (2): "It is a defence ... to prove that his or her absence was a result of circumstances over which he or she had no
-- control."
GIVEN `absent without leave` IS A BOOLEAN, `proved circumstances beyond control` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `guilty of absence without leave` IF
    `absent without leave` AND (NOT `proved circumstances beyond control`)

-- s 23(2): a person deserts if he or she (a) leaves or fails to attend the place of duty "with the intention of remaining
-- permanently absent from duty without lawful authority", or later forms that intention; or (b) "absents himself or herself
-- without leave with intent to avoid service or any particular service before the enemy". No defence like s 22(2) is written.
GIVEN `left or failed to attend place of duty` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `intended to remain permanently absent` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `absent without leave` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `intended to avoid service before the enemy` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `deserts` IF
        (`left or failed to attend place of duty` AND `intended to remain permanently absent`)
    OR  (`absent without leave` AND `intended to avoid service before the enemy`)

-- s 24: knowing another has committed an offence under s 22 or 23, (a) "fails to report the fact without delay; or" (b) "fails to
-- take any steps within his or her power to cause that person to be apprehended". The two limbs are alternatives: doing one is
-- not enough.
GIVEN `knew of an absence or desertion offence` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `reported without delay` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `took steps within power to apprehend` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `guilty of failing to report a deserter or absentee` IF
    `knew of an absence or desertion offence` AND ((NOT `reported without delay`) OR (NOT `took steps within power to apprehend`))

§§ `4, 61 and First Schedule -- summary trial by a disciplinary officer`

-- s 4: a person subject to military law is liable to be tried "by subordinate military courts for any military offence and by a
-- disciplinary officer for any offence specified in the First Schedule". s 61(1): a charge for an offence "not specified in the
-- First Schedule must not be dealt with summarily". The First Schedule lists, among others, disobedience of general orders,
-- absence without leave, failure to report deserters and absentees, conduct to the prejudice of good order, malingering and
-- intoxication. It does NOT list misconduct in action, mutiny, desertion or drugs.
GIVEN `o` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a disciplinary officer may try it summarily` IF
    CONSIDER `o`
    WHEN `disobedience of general orders` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `absence without leave` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `failure to report deserters and absentees` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `conduct to the prejudice of good order` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `malingering` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `intoxication` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `77 -- the right to elect a court martial`

-- s 77(1): a disciplinary officer dealing with a case summarily who "is satisfied on the evidence as to the accused's guilt must,
-- before proceeding to conviction and punishment, other than a reprimand or minor punishment, give the accused an opportunity of
-- electing to be tried by a subordinate military court". (3): if a reprimand or minor punishment is proper, the officer may convict
-- without offering the election.
GIVEN `satisfied of guilt` IS A BOOLEAN, `only a reprimand or minor punishment is proper` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the accused must be offered a court martial` IF
    `satisfied of guilt` AND (NOT `only a reprimand or minor punishment is proper`)

§§ `78 -- time limits for summary trial`

-- s 78(1): no trial by a disciplinary officer (a) where the person was released or discharged within 6 months after the offence --
-- after 3 years from the offence; (b) where the person was an operationally ready national serviceman at the time of the offence
-- -- after 3 years; (c) "in any other case -- after the expiry of 6 months". (2): the Armed Forces Council or a person it
-- authorises "may by written order direct a person to be tried after the expiry of the period of 6 months or 3 years". (3): but
-- never after a time limit in s 111.
GIVEN `released within 6 months of the offence` IS A BOOLEAN, `an ORNS man at the time` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the summary trial limit in months` MEANS
    IF `released within 6 months of the offence` OR `an ORNS man at the time` THEN 36 ELSE 6

GIVEN `released within 6 months of the offence` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `an ORNS man at the time` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `months since the offence` IS A NUMBER,
      `written order to try out of time` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `barred by section 111` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a summary trial is in time` IF
        (    `months since the offence` AT MOST `the summary trial limit in months` `released within 6 months of the offence` `an ORNS man at the time`
         OR  `written order to try out of time`)
    AND (NOT `barred by section 111`)

§§ `111 -- limitation of time for any trial`

-- s 111(1): no trial "unless the trial is started within 3 years after the latest of" (a) the offence; (b) the report to a
-- disciplinary officer or military policeman; (c)-(e) the end of related police investigations, civil proceedings or a civil
-- sentence begun within the default period; (f) arrest under a warrant that could not be executed. The latest date is taken as an
-- input. (2): "This section does not apply to a trial for the offence of misconduct in action, assisting the enemy, mutiny,
-- absence without leave or desertion." (4): but a serviceman who "has served continuously in an exemplary manner for not less
-- than 3 years" must not be tried for desertion (other than desertion on active service) "if that offence was committed more than
-- 3 years before the trial starts".
--
-- So absence without leave has no time limit for a court martial, while desertion -- the graver offence -- has one for an
-- exemplary serviceman, measured from the offence and not from the latest date.
GIVEN `o` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 111 applies` IF
    CONSIDER `o`
    WHEN `misconduct in action` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `mutiny` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `absence without leave` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `desertion` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `o` IS AN Offence,
      `months since the latest date` IS A NUMBER,
      `months since the offence` IS A NUMBER,
      `3 years of exemplary continuous service` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `deserted on active service` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the trial is time-barred` IF
        (`section 111 applies` `o` AND `months since the latest date` GREATER THAN 36)
    OR  (    `o` EQUALS `desertion`
         AND `3 years of exemplary continuous service`
         AND (NOT `deserted on active service`)
         AND `months since the offence` GREATER THAN 36)

§§ `79A -- composition`

-- s 79A(1): an authorised serviceman may compound a First Schedule offence prescribed as compoundable by collecting a sum not
-- exceeding the lower of (a) one half of the maximum fine prescribed for the offence, or one half of the maximum fine a
-- disciplinary officer may impose at summary trial "if lower than the amount in sub-paragraph (i) or if no maximum fine is
-- prescribed"; and (b) $5,000. (2): not where the accused "is in the same chain of command"; (3) the same formation alone is not
-- the same chain of command. (4): on payment, no further proceedings.
GIVEN `x` IS A NUMBER, `y` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the lower of` MEANS IF `x` AT MOST `y` THEN `x` ELSE `y`

GIVEN `a maximum fine is prescribed` IS A BOOLEAN, `the prescribed maximum fine` IS A NUMBER, `the summary maximum fine` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the composition cap` MEANS
    IF `a maximum fine is prescribed`
    THEN `the lower of` 5000 (`the lower of` (`the prescribed maximum fine` DIVIDED BY 2) (`the summary maximum fine` DIVIDED BY 2))
    ELSE `the lower of` 5000 (`the summary maximum fine` DIVIDED BY 2)

GIVEN `o` IS AN Offence, `prescribed as compoundable` IS A BOOLEAN, `same chain of command` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the officer may compound it` IF
    `a disciplinary officer may try it summarily` `o` AND `prescribed as compoundable` AND (NOT `same chain of command`)

§§ `108 -- not to be tried twice`

-- s 108(1): after acquittal or conviction by a subordinate military court (or a conviction quashed on appeal), no second trial by a
-- subordinate military court, "any civil court" or a disciplinary officer for the offence "or for any offence based on the same
-- facts". (2): after acquittal or conviction by a disciplinary officer, no second military trial, "but the person may be tried for
-- the same offence ... by a civil court" which must have regard to any military punishment already undergone. (3): after acquittal
-- or conviction by a civil court, no trial by a subordinate military court or a disciplinary officer.
-- A civil court after a civil court is not this Act's question; the function says only whether THIS ACT bars it.
DECLARE Forum IS ONE OF
    `a subordinate military court`
    `a disciplinary officer`
    `a civil court`

GIVEN `first` IS A Forum, `second` IS A Forum
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `this Act bars the second trial` IF
    CONSIDER `first`
    WHEN `a subordinate military court` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a disciplinary officer` THEN NOT (`second` EQUALS `a civil court`)
    WHEN `a civil court` THEN NOT (`second` EQUALS `a civil court`)

§§ `112(2) -- murder and culpable homicide`

-- s 112(1): a person subject to military law may be tried by a subordinate military court for any offence under Singapore written
-- law. (2): but not "for murder or culpable homicide not amounting to murder unless (a) the person against whom the offence was
-- committed was at the time of the offence subject to military law; or (b) the offence was committed while on active service".
GIVEN `a homicide charge` IS A BOOLEAN, `victim subject to military law` IS A BOOLEAN, `on active service` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a court martial may try the civil offence` IF
    (NOT `a homicide charge`) OR `victim subject to military law` OR `on active service`

§§ `196 -- assignment of military pay and pensions`

-- s 196: every assignment of, charge on, or agreement to assign or charge deferred pay, military reward, or any pension, allowance
-- or relief payable in respect of military service "is void, except so far as the same is made pursuant to any regulations made
-- under this Act for the benefit of the family of the person entitled thereto, or as may be authorised by any written law".
GIVEN `under regulations for the family's benefit` IS A BOOLEAN, `authorised by written law` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the assignment is void` IF
    (NOT `under regulations for the family's benefit`) AND (NOT `authorised by written law`)
